So s[u] = s[v] is possible IFF rank[u+1] < rank[v+1].
Therefore, s[u] < s[v] is forced IFF rank[u] < rank[v] AND rank[u+1] > rank[v+1].

This seems airtight. But wait: what if u+1 or v+1 is out of bounds?
- If u = n (1-indexed) or n-1 (0-indexed): suffix u is length 1. u+1 is empty. rank of empty is -1 (smallest). So rank[u+1] = -1. For any v, rank[u+1] = -1 < rank[v+1] (since rank[v+1] >= 0 or -1). So rank[u+1] < rank[v+1] always holds. Thus s[u] = s[v] is always allowed when u is the last position. No forced strict inequality. My code handles this with `if (u == n-1) continue;`.
- If v = n-1: suffix v is length 1. v+1 is empty, rank[v+1] = -1. rank[u+1] >= 0 > -1. So rank[u+1] > rank[v+1] always holds. Thus s[u] < s[v] is forced. My code handles this with `else if (v == n-1) adj[u].push_back(v);`.

