The longest path in the strict-edge graph can skip elements in SA order. The LDS of the sequence `a` only considers subsequences of the sequence in SA order, which is exactly chains where the vertices are in increasing SA order (which they must be, since rank increases along edges). So any path in the strict-edge graph corresponds to a decreasing subsequence in `a`. And any decreasing subsequence in `a` corresponds to a path in the strict-edge graph (since if a[i] > a[j] for i<j, then rank[SA[i]+1] > rank[SA[j]+1] and rank[SA[i]] < rank[SA[j]], so there's an edge SA[i] -> SA[j]).

So the longest path in the strict-edge graph EQUALS the LDS of `a`. They are the same.

